      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lafayette was named by Mary Miller in 1888 after her late husband, who had moved there to farm land.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lafayette%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1889, a 3‑mile rail spur from Louisville linked Lafayette to the Colorado & Southern Railway, fueling its coal boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lafayette%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Lafayette History Museum preserves old mine tools and houses structures moved from historic coal camps.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lafayette%2C_Colorado"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The public library offers an online oral history collection where residents share personal stories of Lafayette’s past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
